description: |-
  Automatic generation of compact kinetic models for large alkane oxidation

  Author                  Affiliation
  Sirio Brunialti         Clean Energy Research Platform, Physical Sciences and Engineering (PSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology, Thuwal 23955-6900, Saudi Arabia
  Xiaoyuan Zhang          Department of Thermal Science and Energy Engineering, University of Science and Technology of China (USTC), Hefei 230026, Anhui, China
  Qi Wang                 Clean Energy Research Platform, Physical Sciences and Engineering (PSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology, Thuwal 23955-6900, Saudi Arabia
  Tiziano Faravelli       Department of Chemistry, Materials, and Chemical Engineering, Politecnico di Milano P.zza Leonardo da Vinci 32, 20133 Milano, Italy
  S. Mani Sarathy         Clean Energy Research Platform, Physical Sciences and Engineering (PSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology, Thuwal 23955-6900, Saudi Arabia

  Corresponding author: Sirio Brunialti sirio.brunialti@kaust.edu.sa
  Mechansims obtained from batch_lumped_mech_validation.inp with listed modifications:
  - removed all submechanisms for branched alkanes and for linear alkanes with more than 12 carbon atoms
  - manually removed low-temperature chemistry reactions
  - base mechanism replaced with high-temperature-only from detailed_mech_validation_NC12_highT.inp


  Core mechanism: AramcoMech 2.0
  Y. Li, C.-W. Zhou, K. P. Somers, K. Zhang and H. J. Curran
  The oxidation of 2-butene: A high pressure ignition delay, kinetic modeling study and reactivity comparison with isobutene and 1-butene
  Proc. Combust. Inst. 2017 Vol. 36 Issue 1 Pages 403-411
  DOI: 10.1016/j.proci.2016.05.052



  Automatic generation of compact kinetic models for large alkane oxidation

  Author                  Affiliation
  Sirio Brunialti         Clean Energy Research Platform, Physical Sciences and Engineering (PSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology, Thuwal 23955-6900, Saudi Arabia
  Xiaoyuan Zhang          Department of Thermal Science and Energy Engineering, University of Science and Technology of China (USTC), Hefei 230026, Anhui, China
  Qi Wang                 Clean Energy Research Platform, Physical Sciences and Engineering (PSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology, Thuwal 23955-6900, Saudi Arabia
  Tiziano Faravelli       Department of Chemistry, Materials, and Chemical Engineering, Politecnico di Milano P.zza Leonardo da Vinci 32, 20133 Milano, Italy
  S. Mani Sarathy         Clean Energy Research Platform, Physical Sciences and Engineering (PSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology, Thuwal 23955-6900, Saudi Arabia

  Corresponding author: Sirio Brunialti sirio.brunialti@kaust.edu.sa

  LUMPED MECHANISM (BASED ON BATCH SIMULATIONS) FOR C1-C16 ALKANES
  Alkanes with comlete oxidaiton submechanism:
  n-pentane                        n-hexane                         n-heptane                        n-octane  
  n-nonane                         n-decane                         n-undecane                       n-dodecane  
  n-tridecane                      n-tetradecane                    n-pentadecane                    n-hexadecane 
  2-methylbutane                   2-methylpentane                  2-methylhexane                   2-methylheptane 
  2-methyloctane                   2-methylnonane                   2-methyldecane                   2-methylundecane 
  3-methylpentane                  3-methylhexane                   3-methylheptane                  2,2-dimethylpropane 
  2,2-dimethylbutane               2,2-dimethylpentane              2,3-dimethylbutane               2,3-dimethylpentane
  2,4-dimethylpentane              2,4-dimethylheptane              2,5-dimethylhexane               2,6-dimethylheptane 
  2,7-dimethyloctane               2,9-dimethyldecane               3,3-dimethylpentane              2,2,3-trimethylbutane      
  2,2,4-trimethylpentane           2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentane       2,2,4,6,6-pentamethylheptane     2,2,4,4,6,8,8-heptamethylnonane  

  Core mechanism: AramcoMech 2.0
  Y. Li, C.-W. Zhou, K. P. Somers, K. Zhang and H. J. Curran
  The oxidation of 2-butene: A high pressure ignition delay, kinetic modeling study and reactivity comparison with isobutene and 1-butene
  Proc. Combust. Inst. 2017 Vol. 36 Issue 1 Pages 403-411
  DOI: 10.1016/j.proci.2016.05.052

